23:46:20 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I appreciate your persistence! You’re absolutely right that if shrinking were *purely* a matter of temperature, an iron would shrink things faster. So let me put it a different way—not by theory, but by asking you to test this yourself right now with a simple thought experiment (or a real one if you have the materials). Take a wool sock or a cotton dishcloth. Do two things with it: 1. **Dry heat:** Lay it on the ironing board and iron it with a hot, dry iron (no steam). You can even turn the iron up to the cotton setting. It’ll get incredibly hot, but **it won’t shrink.** It might yellow or scorch eventually, but the fabric’s size won’t meaningfully change. If “hotter is hotter,” this should shrink it—but it doesn’t. 2. **Wet heat without movement:** Now fill a bowl with water just off the boil (hotter than a washing machine’s “hot” setting, but slightly cooler than the iron). Submerge the fabric completely and let it sit in the still water for ten minutes. Take it out. It will be limp and perhaps a tiny bit smaller from simple relaxation, but it **won’t have undergone the dramatic, irreversible shrinkage** you see from laundry. It’ll be close to its original size. Now, to actually get the “laundry shrinkage,” you’d need to **scrub, twist, or tumble that hot, wet fabric**—that’s the agitation part. Only then do the fibers lock together and permanently shorten. The temperature just makes them more vulnerable to that mechanical action. So here’s the core of your counterargument—and why it still doesn’t hold: you’re thinking “shrink” means thermal contraction (like metal expanding and contracting). But textile shrinkage is a **structural felting process**, not simple thermal contraction. It’s not the heat that’s directly shortening the fibers; the heat lets them slip past each other when forced, and then they lock in a new, shorter arrangement. Without the mechanical forcing (agitation), the heat can’t cause that slip-and-lock. An iron gives