23:48:29 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I hear you, and I understand that you’re holding firm to the idea that heat alone should be the cause. But the simple truth is that it isn’t—and I know that can be frustrating when it seems to contradict common sense. Here’s the crux of it: Laundry shrinkage is not just a heat reaction. It’s a physical process that requires three things: - Heat (to relax fibers) - Water (to swell them and make them slippery) - Agitation (the rubbing, tumbling, or wringing that forces the swollen fibers to lock together in a shrunken state) A hot iron provides only one of those three—heat. It deliberately avoids the water and the agitation. No waterlogging, no rubbing. So even though the iron is hotter, it’s missing the other two essential ingredients. That’s why it doesn’t shrink clothes. I get why you feel hotter should mean more shrinkage, but in this case, the mechanism matters more than the temperature. It’s similar to how a spark can ignite gasoline fumes, but holding a blowtorch to liquid gasoline won’t make it explode—because you need the right mixture (vapor + oxygen), not just heat. If you still aren’t convinced, I’d genuinely encourage you to try the experiment: take an old wool sock, measure it, iron it dry on the hottest setting, and measure again. It won’t shrink. That’s the only way to see past the “heat’s heat” assumption. You’re welcome to disagree—I’m just here to provide the explanation and the evidence. If you’d like to dig deeper into any part of the chemistry or physics, I’m happy to. But I’ve said all I can on the core question, so I’ll leave it there.
